    fcd_title: "How are these numbers calculated?",
    fcd_intro_title: "Where these numbers come from",
    fcd_intro_body:
      "Every formula below uses your business's real numbers for this period ({period}), not a generic example — so you can confirm the result is correct.",
    fcd_period_fallback: "all your imported sales",
    fcd_cogs_title: "1. COGS (Cost of Goods Sold)",
    fcd_cogs_body:
      "Initial inventory for the period, plus what you purchased, minus what was left at the end. It's what was REALLY consumed, regardless of whether it was sold, spoiled, or lost.",
    fcd_cogs_no_data:
      "There aren't two inventory snapshots (initial and final) for this period — your current inventory was used as an approximation for both endpoints.",
    fcd_realcost_title: "2. Real Food Cost",
    fcd_realcost_body: "COGS divided by total sales for the period.",
    fcd_theoretical_title: "3. Theoretical Food Cost",
    fcd_theoretical_body:
      "What it SHOULD have cost, based on the cost of each recipe sheet multiplied by how many of each dish were sold — regardless of waste or losses.",
    fcd_theoretical_formula_note: "sum of recipe sheet cost × quantity sold, per dish",
    fcd_variance_title: "4. Variance",
    fcd_variance_body:
      "The difference between what it actually cost and what it should have cost. A high positive number indicates waste, theft, or a counting error not reflected in your recipe sheets.",
    fcd_primecost_title: "5. Prime Cost",
    fcd_primecost_body:
      "COGS plus labor cost for the period. Your labor cost is configured monthly for the business, so it's first prorated to the exact days in this period ({days} days).",
    fcd_margin_title: "6. Contribution Margin",
    fcd_margin_body:
      "Total sales minus total theoretical cost (based on recipe sheets). What's left to cover your fixed expenses and generate profit.",
    fcd_margin_note:
      "Per dish, it's the same calculation: sale price − theoretical cost of that recipe sheet. That's how the \"Per-dish detail\" table and the best/worst margin charts are built.",
    fcd_turnover_title: "7. Inventory Turnover",
    fcd_turnover_body:
      "How many times your entire inventory \"turned over\" during the period: COGS divided by average inventory (initial and final).",
    fcd_pnl_title: "8. Simplified Income Statement (P&L)",
    fcd_pnl_body:
      "Sales minus all your expenses (COGS plus the fixed expenses configured for the business, each prorated to {days} days just like the labor cost above).",
    fcd_pnl_line_labor: "Labor Cost",
    fcd_pnl_line_rent: "Rent",
    fcd_pnl_line_utilities: "Utilities",
    fcd_pnl_line_operational: "Operating Costs",
    fcd_pnl_line_other: "Other Expenses",
    fcd_suppliervar_title: "9. Supplier Price Variance",
    fcd_suppliervar_body:
      "Compares each ingredient's most recent purchase price against its historical weighted average cost (the same one your recipe sheets use). Only variances of 5% or more are shown.",
    fcd_suppliervar_empty: "No ingredient has a variance of 5% or more right now, which is why the list is empty.",
    fcd_menueng_title: "10. Menu Engineering",
    fcd_menueng_body:
      "Each dish is compared against the average of your own menu (not a fixed number) along two axes: how much it sold and how much contribution margin it left.",
